WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.200 position:63%
Power bills just had the fastest&nbsp;
period of price growth on record. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.400 position:63%
it’s because of demand… that doesn’t even exist?

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.000 position:63%
On America’s largest power grid, $21.3 billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:16.400 position:63%
almost half of what customers paid&nbsp;
to reserve three years of power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:22.500 position:63%
was for data centers… that hadn’t been built&nbsp;
yet. And many of them likely never will.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.300 position:63%
But that’s just the beginning. Data center&nbsp;
developers have asked for 83% of everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.800 position:63%
America’s power plants can produce. Less&nbsp;
than a third of it will likely ever get used.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.600 position:63%
Data centers completely broke how the US grids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.800 position:63%
works. And now US power is&nbsp;
in the midst of a crisis.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.700 position:63%
So how does a data center&nbsp;
that doesn’t exist… or will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.000 position:63%
never exist end up on your power&nbsp;
bill? How did this even happen?

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.400 position:63%
The most important thing to understand is&nbsp;
that power doesn’t get charged the way you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:07.900 position:63%
probably expect. Most people assume their power&nbsp;
bill… is just the power they used. But, a big,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:13.200 position:63%
growing chunk of it, is from something called&nbsp;
capacity. And it’s actually pretty simple.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.100 position:63%
On your power bill, you pay for three important&nbsp;
costs, which are usually the biggest. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:24.600 position:63%
the delivery, like the cost of poles, wires, and&nbsp;
local lines, Second, the power you actually use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.800 position:63%
and a third charge, which is a surprising&nbsp;
amount of your bill: capacity charges.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:35.300 position:63%
So… what the heck is this “capacity”?&nbsp;
Well, it shows us how power bills have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:39.700 position:63%
skyrocketed… by reserving power&nbsp;
for demand… that doesn’t exist.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:45.000 position:63%
Demand for power fluctuates. The hottest day of&nbsp;
the year, when everyone’s using their AC units,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.400 position:63%
will use far more power than any regular day.&nbsp;
Same goes for the coldest day. But, you still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.200 position:63%
need to have power capacity at the plants,&nbsp;
for when that sudden spike in energy comes.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.893 position:63%
So, people pay for that extra capacity, to have&nbsp;
power plants running, even if much of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.893 --> 00:02:06.493 position:63%
capacity, isn’t used much of the year. So, a&nbsp;
capacity charge. But what’s really interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:10.893 position:63%
is how that price is set, which comes&nbsp;
with a problem we’re discovering now.

00:02:10.893 --> 00:02:15.893 position:63%
On the eastern grid, it’s set by a company&nbsp;
called PJM, which runs the power grid for about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.893 --> 00:02:22.393 position:63%
67 million people in 13 states and Washington&nbsp;
DC. It’s the nation’s largest grid operator.

00:02:22.393 --> 00:02:26.993 position:63%
PJM forecasts how much power the grid&nbsp;
will need on its busiest day. Then it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.993 --> 00:02:31.093 position:63%
holds an auction to buy capacity&nbsp;
from power companies. Basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.093 --> 00:02:36.093 position:63%
a promise from power plants to be available&nbsp;
that day, and each plant names its price.

00:02:36.093 --> 00:02:40.993 position:63%
PJM accepts the offers from cheapest to most&nbsp;
expensive, until the supply they forecast is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:48.393 position:63%
met. So, Plant A offers 40 units at $50. Plant&nbsp;
B offers 40 at $150. Plant C offers 40 at $325.

00:02:48.393 --> 00:02:54.293 position:63%
If PJM needs 100 units, it buys 40 units from&nbsp;
Plant A, 40 units from Plant B, and 20 units&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.293 --> 00:02:59.993 position:63%
from Plant C, to reach 100. Each gets paid at&nbsp;
the price of the highest accepted bid. So A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.993 --> 00:03:05.593 position:63%
B, and C get paid at a rate of $325, and&nbsp;
that cost goes onto everyone’s power bill.

00:03:05.593 --> 00:03:10.593 position:63%
So in a way, you’re paying for the power you use,&nbsp;
but also, paying power companies to have extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.593 --> 00:03:15.993 position:63%
capacity available, even if it’s not used most of&nbsp;
the the time. You don’t want to not have power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.993 --> 00:03:20.093 position:63%
when demand spikes. Otherwise you&nbsp;
get a blackout. Which is much worse.

00:03:20.093 --> 00:03:23.193 position:63%
But, this capacity cost has been exploding.

00:03:23.193 --> 00:03:28.993 position:63%
The 2023 PJM capacity price by&nbsp;
auction was about $29 per MW per day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.993 --> 00:03:35.393 position:63%
But in the following auction, the auction&nbsp;
price jumped almost ten times, to about $269!

00:03:35.393 --> 00:03:40.593 position:63%
This was so high, that, after Pennsylvania&nbsp;
Governor Josh Shapiro filed a complaint with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.593 --> 00:03:45.493 position:63%
the federal energy regulator, a price ceiling&nbsp;
was set, with minor adjustments each year. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.493 --> 00:03:49.593 position:63%
it’s a good thing they did, because&nbsp;
the capacity prices continued to grow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.593 --> 00:03:53.793 position:63%
and in 2026, it was the third auction&nbsp;
in a row to reach the price cap.

00:03:53.793 --> 00:03:58.693 position:63%
But, it gets worse. Included in&nbsp;
that price is PJM’s safety cushion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.693 --> 00:04:03.294 position:63%
as they aim to have roughly 20% more power&nbsp;
available than it forecasts for the busiest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.294 --> 00:04:08.294 position:63%
hour. This is to prevent any blackouts if&nbsp;
actual demand goes beyond the forecast.

00:04:08.294 --> 00:04:12.494 position:63%
Well, both the December 2025&nbsp;
auction, and the July 2026 auction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.494 --> 00:04:16.394 position:63%
were short of this cushion. The&nbsp;
first time this has happened.

00:04:16.394 --> 00:04:21.394 position:63%
Even paying the highest price allowed, PJM&nbsp;
couldn't find enough power plants to cover its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:26.494 position:63%
own forecast with a safety margin. The grid&nbsp;
has less room for a heat wave or cold snap.

00:04:26.494 --> 00:04:32.294 position:63%
So why have capacity prices skyrocketed? Why is&nbsp;
there a sudden need for all this power capacity?…

00:04:33.194 --> 00:04:36.494 position:63%
Data centers. A whole lot of data centers.

00:04:36.494 --> 00:04:40.094 position:63%
But, the problem isn’t necessarily&nbsp;
the data centers themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.094 --> 00:04:41.994 position:63%
but how they’ve broken the existing system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.994 --> 00:04:48.794 position:63%
altogether. Much of this capacity comes from data&nbsp;
centers that don’t exist, or never will exist.

00:04:56.694 --> 00:05:00.994 position:63%
In Northern Virginia, where there’s a huge&nbsp;
cluster of data centers, the busiest summer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.994 --> 00:05:08.094 position:63%
day of 2025’s actual use was 23% higher&nbsp;
than in 2019. Winter was also 45% higher.

00:05:08.094 --> 00:05:15.994 position:63%
But that’s the actual use. And… 23%? Even&nbsp;
45%? That doesn’t explain the massive jump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.994 --> 00:05:22.194 position:63%
in capacity prices. What does, though, is how&nbsp;
PJM forecasts what will be needed down the line.

00:05:22.194 --> 00:05:28.694 position:63%
Across three auctions, 45% of what customers&nbsp;
paid for standby power, $21.3 billion, was for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:34.894 position:63%
data centers that only existed in the forecast.&nbsp;
That’s almost half. Almost half the standby bill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.894 --> 00:05:40.194 position:63%
was for buildings that weren't there. And those&nbsp;
are charges… that end up on people’s bills.

00:05:40.194 --> 00:05:44.794 position:63%
“This is not something the data centers&nbsp;
are actually paying themselves. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.794 --> 00:05:48.894 position:63%
is a cost being imposed on all&nbsp;
customers in the PJM footprint.”

00:05:48.894 --> 00:05:52.394 position:63%
So, how the heck are data centers&nbsp;
increasing the cost… if they don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.394 --> 00:05:56.094 position:63%
exist? Well, it comes down to how&nbsp;
the queue for utilities works.

00:05:56.094 --> 00:05:59.894 position:63%
A developer who’s planning one data&nbsp;
center, wants to get it done as fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.894 --> 00:06:04.094 position:63%
as possible. But connecting to the&nbsp;
grid, is actually the slowest part.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.094 --> 00:06:08.095 position:63%
Building it can be done in a year, but&nbsp;
connecting it can take multiple years.

00:06:08.096 --> 00:06:11.796 position:63%
So, developers ask a bunch&nbsp;
of utility companies at once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.796 --> 00:06:16.496 position:63%
to see who can connect them the fastest. This&nbsp;
is called “service territory shopping.” It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.496 --> 00:06:19.796 position:63%
also helps figure out who’s the&nbsp;
cheapest, and provides backups.

00:06:20.596 --> 00:06:23.696 position:63%
“The data center people are&nbsp;
shopping these projects around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.696 --> 00:06:27.496 position:63%
and maybe they approach five or more&nbsp;
utilities. They’re only going to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.496 --> 00:06:31.696 position:63%
one data center. But if all five utilities&nbsp;
think that interest is going to lead to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.696 --> 00:06:35.096 position:63%
data center, they’re going to build&nbsp;
way more capacity than is needed.”

00:06:36.296 --> 00:06:41.496 position:63%
It’s not just speed, though. It’s also to hold&nbsp;
a place in the queue to be connected. So, a data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.496 --> 00:06:46.696 position:63%
center, sitting in line to be connected, which&nbsp;
doesn’t really exist… drives up power prices…

00:06:46.696 --> 00:06:47.196 position:63%
Yeah.

00:06:47.496 --> 00:06:51.796 position:63%
Now, imagine not just one developer doing&nbsp;
that… but a lot of data centers doing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.796 --> 00:06:55.896 position:63%
across a state. Suddenly, you have&nbsp;
all these requests, from data centers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.896 --> 00:07:01.496 position:63%
to all these utility providers… and it starts&nbsp;
to look like a surge in need for capacity. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.496 --> 00:07:06.296 position:63%
the grid needs to ensure they can produce&nbsp;
enough power, and change the future forecast.

00:07:06.296 --> 00:07:11.496 position:63%
This has become a huge problem. But&nbsp;
these aren’t public requests. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.496 --> 00:07:15.796 position:63%
made privately to different providers, who&nbsp;
might not validate with one another. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.796 --> 00:07:19.796 position:63%
the big picture looks like there’s a&nbsp;
sudden, colossal wave of new demand…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.796 --> 00:07:24.296 position:63%
even if there isn’t. And the sheer&nbsp;
scale of this, is kind of mind-boggling.

00:07:24.296 --> 00:07:29.896 position:63%
Across the US, data center developers&nbsp;
have asked for 1,066 gigawatts of power.

00:07:29.896 --> 00:07:33.096 position:63%
… that is 83% of the combined output,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.096 --> 00:07:41.396 position:63%
of every power plant in the US. 83%! Over 80%&nbsp;
of all the commercial power the US can produce.

00:07:41.396 --> 00:07:48.296 position:63%
In reality, only about 28% of that demand, will&nbsp;
actually be used. And this is hugely inflating the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.296 --> 00:07:54.596 position:63%
overall forecast of data center demand. But even&nbsp;
if they’re not real, they get added to the cost.

00:07:54.596 --> 00:07:58.796 position:63%
From the last three capacity auctions, the&nbsp;
costs related to data center forecasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.796 --> 00:08:04.396 position:63%
not existing data centers, were&nbsp;
$21.3 billion. 45% of the total cost.

00:08:04.396 --> 00:08:09.797 position:63%
So, what are we doing about it? Well,&nbsp;
there’s some good news… and some bad news.

00:08:17.297 --> 00:08:20.097 position:63%
If you’ve watched this far, consider subscribing!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.097 --> 00:08:22.997 position:63%
It helps the channel and means&nbsp;
you won’t miss future videos.

00:08:23.697 --> 00:08:28.397 position:63%
American Electric Power Ohio, or “AEP,”&nbsp;
the state's largest power company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.397 --> 00:08:32.897 position:63%
got requests from 50 data center customers,&nbsp;
wanting to draw a combined 30 gigawatts at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.897 --> 00:08:36.897 position:63%
once. Roughly three times New York&nbsp;
City’s peak electricity demand.

00:08:37.497 --> 00:08:38.497 position:63%
Insane.

00:08:38.497 --> 00:08:43.497 position:63%
So in July 2025, Ohio regulators&nbsp;
made one simple change. Before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.497 --> 00:08:48.197 position:63%
a developer could ask for power, and just sit&nbsp;
on the waiting list, without signing anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.197 --> 00:08:52.997 position:63%
or paying anything. They were only charged&nbsp;
once they formally signed a contract. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.997 --> 00:08:56.497 position:63%
waiting in line for multiple utility&nbsp;
companies didn't cost anything.

00:08:56.497 --> 00:09:00.697 position:63%
Now, every data center on the list had&nbsp;
to commit by a deadline and commit to 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.697 --> 00:09:06.997 position:63%
years of power. And pay for 85% of the power&nbsp;
they reserved, whether they used it or not.

00:09:06.997 --> 00:09:09.297 position:63%
After the change, the 30 requested gigawatts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.297 --> 00:09:14.397 position:63%
dropped down to just 5.6. Roughly&nbsp;
80% of the waiting list disappeared.

00:09:14.997 --> 00:09:19.897 position:63%
It’s not just Ohio either. Utility providers&nbsp;
“serving eight of the 10 largest data centre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.897 --> 00:09:24.497 position:63%
hotspots — including Virginia, Illinois&nbsp;
and Texas — [are] overhauling their pricing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.497 --> 00:09:27.897 position:63%
structures and setting stringent&nbsp;
rules for prospective projects.”

00:09:27.897 --> 00:09:30.797 position:63%
We’re also seeing new power&nbsp;
capacity being approved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.797 --> 00:09:35.997 position:63%
like under Georgia Power. But even if they&nbsp;
primarily serve data centers, “the costs of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.997 --> 00:09:40.597 position:63%
providing service to new data centers will&nbsp;
not be passed on to current ratepayers.”

00:09:40.597 --> 00:09:44.297 position:63%
Even if “projected data center&nbsp;
demand does not materialize.”

00:09:44.297 --> 00:09:50.697 position:63%
Which is fantastic. But, there’s still a problem.&nbsp;
Prices unfortunately won’t come down soon.

00:09:50.697 --> 00:09:56.997 position:63%
PJM forecasts capacity prices three years ahead&nbsp;
of time. In July 2026… the forecast is for June&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.997 --> 00:10:03.197 position:63%
2028 through to May 2029. Even though the rules&nbsp;
for data center requests are being tackled now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.197 --> 00:10:06.697 position:63%
there is still this long tail to deal&nbsp;
with, for a few more years at least.

00:10:06.697 --> 00:10:11.798 position:63%
Data centers are a new problem for the grid&nbsp;
to manage. But even if a huge chunk of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.798 --> 00:10:16.598 position:63%
is just requests, there’s still a huge gap&nbsp;
between what we need, and where we are now.

00:10:16.598 --> 00:10:20.598 position:63%
There’s a major difference between&nbsp;
data centers and homes. Homes spike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.598 --> 00:10:23.998 position:63%
though at consistent periods.&nbsp;
Certain times of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.998 --> 00:10:28.698 position:63%
and during certain seasons. From when everyone&nbsp;
gets home from work, until they head to bed.

00:10:28.698 --> 00:10:33.098 position:63%
But data centers run flat out, at all times. 24/7.

00:10:33.098 --> 00:10:36.598 position:63%
This has “shifted our risk into&nbsp;
the winter period even more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.598 --> 00:10:39.998 position:63%
because it’s sort of bringing the&nbsp;
winter peak closer to the summer peak.”

00:10:39.998 --> 00:10:45.798 position:63%
AI is impacting all kinds of industries&nbsp;
and businesses, and not just the grid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.798 --> 00:10:49.998 position:63%
Small businesses, for example, seem to&nbsp;
be obsessed with using it. For posters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.998 --> 00:10:53.998 position:63%
menus, and social media. And a&nbsp;
lot of it doesn't look great.

00:10:53.998 --> 00:10:59.198 position:63%
So, why are these AI posters everywhere?&nbsp;
Even the ones that look… awful.

00:10:59.198 --> 00:11:01.198 position:63%
To find out, watch this video next.

